wspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ\left(S_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+S_{u}\mathbf{u},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},H_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+H_{u}\mathbf{u}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E20Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE\left(S_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+S_{u}u,u\right)\leq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE\left(S_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+S_{u}u,u\right)\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p43.1" class="ltx_p">DeePC (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) can handle mildly nonlinear and/or time-varying behavior. However, for strongly nonlinear or time-varying systems, one needs online DeePC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib135" title="" class="ltx_ref">135</a>, <a href="#bib.bib136" title="" class="ltx_ref">136</a>, <a href="#bib.bib137" title="" class="ltx_ref">137</a>, <a href="#bib.bib138" title="" class="ltx_ref">138</a>]</cite>, which updates the Hankel matrix using real-time data. In both formulations, (<a href="#S3.E13" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), DeePC reduces to a quadratic program (QP) under a quadratic cost function, and thus requires an online QP solver to compute the optimal control input via the decision variable <math id="S3.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. If the constraint <math id="S3.p43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is absent, the problem becomes unconstrained DeePC, which admits a closed-form solution with significantly reduced computational burden. In this case, the control input is given by <math id="S3.p43.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=U_{F}\mathbf{g}=K_{d}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{d}^{ini}w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐠</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=U_{F}\mathbf{g}=K_{d}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{d}^{ini}w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a closed-form solution essentially exists only for convex quadratic problems with equality constraints. For more general optimization problems, a closed form is typically unavailable, and DeePC must be solved numerically. This can lead to high computational cost because the dimension of the decision variable <math id="S3.p43.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> scales with the data length <math id="S3.p43.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hankel matrix. In addition, DeePC requires storing the Hankel matrix <math id="S3.p43.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can become very large for long datasets, leading to substantial memory demands. This poses a fundamental limitation: while more data typically improves representation of the nonlinear system (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), it also makes DeePC increasingly expensive to implement. To address this trade-off, several efficient variants have been proposed to improve the scalability and practicality of DeePC.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We review and compare eight approaches to improve the efficiency of the data-driven optimal policies while preserving their performance guarantees. Although the primary focus is on DeePC, most of the methods are also applicable to MPC, ML-based MPC, RL, MPC-based RL, and LLM agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subspace Predictive Control:</span>
In subspace predictive control (SPC), the state-space model (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is replaced by an algebraic relation that relates the future output trajectory <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> with the initial trajectory <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and the future input signal <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. Within the DeePC framework, SPC retains the Fundamental Lemma as a hard constraint in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), isolates <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=Y_{F}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=Y_{F}g</annotation></semantics></math> from it, and constructs an ARX predictor for <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib139" title="" class="ltx_ref">139</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex60" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex60X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex60X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g=\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\\
u\end{bmatrix}=H_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+H_{u}u," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g=\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\\
u\end{bmatrix}=H_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+H_{u}u,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex61" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex61X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex61X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex61X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=Y_{F}\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\\
u\end{bmatrix}=S_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+S_{u}u," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=Y_{F}\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\\
u\end{bmatrix}=S_{ini}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+S_{u}u,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <sup id="S4.p2.4.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> represents the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse operator, <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{ini}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(m+p)T_{ini}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{ini}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(m+p)T_{ini}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times mN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times mN}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ini}\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times(m+p)T_{ini}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ini}\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times(m+p)T_{ini}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times mN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{pN\times mN}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, using the above equations, one can replace DeePC (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) with SPC (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>), where the fixed data matrices <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are identified, from the collected data, offline. Then, substituting the ARX predictor into the cost function and the safety constraint, one can rewrite (<a href="#S3.E19" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) as (<a href="#S3.E20" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>). For the cost function (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), the problem is a QP in <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,\sigma_{u},\sigma_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,\sigma_{u},\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. Eliminating <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> in closed form yields a QP in <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> only <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib140" title="" class="ltx_ref">140</a>]</cite>. Solving the tracking problem then requires an online QP solver for the decision variable <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the computational burden depends on the dimension of <math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mN</annotation></semantics></math>, instead of the dimension of <math id="S4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, we only need to save <math id="S4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> (we can skip the last term of the cost function in SPC), which requires much less memory compared to saving the Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> since they do not depend on the length of the collected data <math id="S4.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if the constraint <math id="S4.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is neglected, the problem is referred to as unconstrained SPC, and one can achieve a closed-form solution by setting the gradient of the quadratic cost to zero <math id="S4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J_{u}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J_{u}=0)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=K_{s}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{s}^{ini}w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=K_{s}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{s}^{ini}w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E21X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{z}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{z},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ\left(Y_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right),U_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right),\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐳</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐮</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{z}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{z},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ\left(Y_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right),U_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right),\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E21Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE\left(Y_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right),U_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right)\right)\leq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE\left(Y_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right),U_{F}\left(\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}\\
y_{ini}+\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi\mathbf{z}\right)\right)\leq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Although SPC reduces the computational cost of DeePC, it does not qualify as a fully direct data-driven control method, since the predictor matrices <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> are identified offline via least-squares estimation. Furthermore, because SPC relies on a linear predictor, it is not well-suited for addressing the nonlinear system (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). For the deterministic LTI system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), ML-based MPC, DeePC, and SPC coincide and yield the same <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Beyond this case, they differ since ML-based MPC and SPC allow any <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and predict <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> using the parametric model and the ARX predictor, respectively; however, DeePC restricts <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=U_{F}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=U_{F}g</annotation></semantics></math> and predicts <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=Y_{F}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=Y_{F}g</annotation></semantics></math>. This distinction reflects a bias–variance trade-off. ML-based MPC and SPC reduce variance by relying on a fixed linear predictor, which enables them to handle measurement noise in the linear system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) effectively. Yet, when the true system is nonlinear (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), this fixed predictor incurs model bias, leading to systematic prediction error. The restriction lowers variance, as it limits the degrees of freedom and prevents overfitting to noise, but increases bias if the feasible set is too small. By contrast, DeePC does not commit to a single linear parametric model, which reduces model bias when the system departs from linearity. This flexibility allows DeePC to perform better on nonlinear systems compared to ML-based MPC with linear models or SPC. The downside, however, is greater sensitivity to noise, which is mitigated by regularization and slack penalties.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nullspace Predictive Control:</span>
This framework was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib141" title="" class="ltx_ref">141</a>]</cite>, although the authors did not assign it a specific name. We refer to it as nullspace predictive control (NPC), since it exploits the null-space representation of the Past submatrix in the Fundamental Lemma. Within the DeePC formulation (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), the decision variable <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into a lower-dimensional variable, allowing the future trajectory <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,y)</annotation></semantics></math> to be computed with reduced online computational cost. In analogy to (<a href="#S3.E14" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), NPC enforces the Past component of the Fundamental Lemma as a hard constraint in (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) and represents the null-space parametrization of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex62" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex62X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex62X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex62X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g=\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi z=\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi z," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g=\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi z=\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+\Phi z,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(m+p)T_{ini}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(m+p)T_{ini}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(L-r_{p})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(L-r_{p})}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-r_{p}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-r_{p}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}=rank(\mathcal{H}_{P})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}=rank(\mathcal{H}_{P})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a basis of the kernel (null space) of <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{P}\Phi=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{P}\Phi=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, one has</p>
<table id="S4.Ex63" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex63X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex63X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}g=\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}\Phi z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}g=\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}^{\dagger}\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\end{bmatrix}\Phi z</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex63Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex63Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="3.408em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}+\sigma_{u}\\
y_{ini}+\sigma_{y}\end{bmatrix}+0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Both <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed offline using standard linear algebra routines. This decomposition allows one to express the future trajectory <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,y)</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of the lower-dimensional variable <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, which is useful in the online phase of DeePC. Therefore, one can rewrite DeePC (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) as NPC (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). If <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}=L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}=L</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> disappears; thus, NPC collapses to DeePC. For the cost function (<a href="#S3.E16" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), the problem is a QP in <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(z,\sigma_{u},\sigma_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(z,\sigma_{u},\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly to SPC, one can solve out <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> in closed form and get a QP in <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> only, which requires an online QP solver to solve the tracking problem for the optimization variable <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the computational burden depends on the dimension of <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-r_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-r_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, instead of the dimension of <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. However, NPC can easily be bigger/slower than SPC since the absolute reduction <math id="S4.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is a small fraction of <math id="S4.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S4.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is large, and we need an efficient DeePC, NPC cuts only a small slice off <math id="S4.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, considering their memory burden, storing and using <math id="S4.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math>, which depend on the length of the collected data <math id="S4.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, is typically much larger than <math id="S4.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. It should be noted that if the constraint <math id="S4.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is neglected, the problem is referred to as unconstrained NPC, and one can achieve a closed-form solution by setting the gradient of the quadratic cost to zero <math id="S4.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J_{z}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J_{z}=0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting online controller is a linear feedback of the form <math id="S4.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=K_{n}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{n}^{ini}w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=K_{n}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{n}^{ini}w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is obtained from <math id="S4.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, the constrained NPC problem requires an online QP solver, which has a high computational cost since the dimension of the decision variable <math id="S4.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> can be large as it depends on the number of samples <math id="S4.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> used in the Hankel matrix. It is worth noting that in general convex optimization problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib140" title="" class="ltx_ref">140</a>]</cite> and nonconvex optimization problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib142" title="" class="ltx_ref">142</a>]</cite>, although <math id="S4.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> can be eliminated, retaining them explicitly as decision variables is often preferable for numerical stability and theoretical guarantees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">NPC is a change of variables that reduces the dimensionality of the DeePC problem without altering its statistical properties. Since the feasible set remains unchanged, NPC preserves DeePC’s bias–variance behavior. In particular, computing <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{P}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> offline merely precomputes linear algebra operations that would otherwise be performed online, without modifying the underlying optimization problem. Unlike SPC, which defines a different feasible set through regression-based identification of predictor matrices, NPC enforces the same Hankel feasibility constraints as DeePC. Consequently, DeePC and NPC yield identical feasible pairs <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and therefore the same optimizer <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{*},y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{*},y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math>; only the representation of the optimization variables differs.
Finally, while DeePC and NPC are equivalent in this sense, ML-based MPC with nonlinear models (e.g., DNNs, CNNs, RNNs) can surpass both in nonlinear settings. Nonetheless, recent online DeePC formulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib135" title="" class="ltx_ref">135</a>, <a href="#bib.bib136" title="" class="ltx_ref">136</a>, <a href="#bib.bib137" title="" class="ltx_ref">137</a>, <a href="#bib.bib138" title="" class="ltx_ref">138</a>]</cite> are emerging as direct data-driven counterparts to such nonlinear ML-based MPC approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduced-Order Data-Enabled Predictive Control:</span>
The Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> may have a large dimension due to the required collected data length <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. To address this, one can apply singular value decomposition (SVD) and retain only the dominant singular components, thereby constructing a reduced-order Hankel matrix. This approach not only mitigates the effect of noise in trajectory data but also reduces computational complexity and memory requirements for data-driven control methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib143" title="" class="ltx_ref">143</a>]</cite>. Building on this idea, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib144" title="" class="ltx_ref">144</a>]</cite> incorporates SVD directly into the DeePC framework by defining a reduced-order Hankel matrix within the Fundamental Lemma. Considering <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\mathrm{rank}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\mathrm{rank}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, one can formulate the SVD of the Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex64" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex64X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex64X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex64X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}=U\Sigma V^{\top}=[U_{r}\hskip 5.69054ptU_{qK-r}]\begin{bmatrix}\Sigma_{r}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}[V_{r}\hskip 5.69054ptV_{L-r}]^{\top}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}=U\Sigma V^{\top}=[U_{r}\hskip 5.69054ptU_{qK-r}]\begin{bmatrix}\Sigma_{r}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}[V_{r}\hskip 5.69054ptV_{L-r}]^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=m+p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=m+p</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the singular matrix, and <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times qK}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times qK}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> are left and right singular vectors, respectively, such that <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="UU^{\top}=U^{\top}U=I_{qK}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">UU^{\top}=U^{\top}U=I_{qK}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VV^{\top}=V^{\top}V=I_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VV^{\top}=V^{\top}V=I_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the top <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> non-zero singular values, <math id="S4.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{qK-r}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times(qK-r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{qK-r}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times(qK-r)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{L-r}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(L-r)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{L-r}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times(L-r)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, one has</p>
<table id="S4.Ex65" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex65X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex65X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex65X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}g=U_{r}\Sigma_{r}V_{r}^{\top}g=\mathcal{H}^{\prime}g^{\prime}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}g=U_{r}\Sigma_{r}V_{r}^{\top}g=\mathcal{H}^{\prime}g^{\prime},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}=U_{r}\Sigma_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}=U_{r}\Sigma_{r}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}=V_{r}^{\top}g\in\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}=V_{r}^{\top}g\in\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the pre-collected data is sufficient rich, then we have <math id="S4.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=mK+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=mK+n</annotation></semantics></math> for the deterministic LTI system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and <math id="S4.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mK+n\leq r\leq\mathrm{min}(qK,L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mK+n\leq r\leq\mathrm{min}(qK,L)</annotation></semantics></math> for the general nonlinear system (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Thus, one can approximate the Hankel matrix using a rank order <math id="S4.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mK+n\leq r_{a}\leq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mK+n\leq r_{a}\leq r</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex66" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex66X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex66X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex66X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}g\approx U_{r_{a}}\Sigma_{r_{a}}V_{r_{a}}^{\top}g=\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}g^{\prime\prime}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}g\approx U_{r_{a}}\Sigma_{r_{a}}V_{r_{a}}^{\top}g=\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}g^{\prime\prime},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.7" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p7.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}=U_{r_{a}}\Sigma_{r_{a}}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times r_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}=U_{r_{a}}\Sigma_{r_{a}}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times r_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime\prime}=V_{r_{a}}^{\top}g\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime\prime}=V_{r_{a}}^{\top}g\in\mathbb{R}^{r_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is worth noting that we have <math id="S4.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}g=\mathcal{H}^{\prime}g^{\prime}=\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}g^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}g=\mathcal{H}^{\prime}g^{\prime}=\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}g^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a}=r</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Now, one can formulate a reduced-order DeePC as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E22X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{g^{\prime\prime}}^{*},\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{g^{\prime\prime}},\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{g^{\prime\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>𝐠</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐠</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">′′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐮</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐮</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{g^{\prime\prime}}^{*},\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{g^{\prime\prime}},\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{g^{\prime\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E22Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}U^{\prime\prime}_{P}\\
Y^{\prime\prime}_{P}\\
U^{\prime\prime}_{F}\\
Y^{\prime\prime}_{F}\end{bmatrix}g^{\prime\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}U^{\prime\prime}_{P}\\
Y^{\prime\prime}_{P}\\
U^{\prime\prime}_{F}\\
Y^{\prime\prime}_{F}\end{bmatrix}g^{\prime\prime}=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E22Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E22Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E(y,u)\leq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E(y,u)\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">where the matrices <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime\prime}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime\prime}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime\prime}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime\prime}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime\prime}_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime\prime}_{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{\prime\prime}_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{\prime\prime}_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained offline from <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly to (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), the problem is reformulated with <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as the sole optimization variable. Consequently, the computational burden now depends on the dimension of <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than on the dimension of <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, only the reduced Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> needs to be stored, requiring significantly less memory than the full Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reduced-order DeePC is solved in exactly the same way as DeePC, and one can achieve a closed-form solution <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=K_{r}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{r}^{ini}w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=K_{r}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{r}^{ini}w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> for the unconstrained QP case by setting the gradient of the quadratic cost to zero <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J_{g^{\prime\prime}}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J_{g^{\prime\prime}}=0)</annotation></semantics></math>. In terms of complexity, SPC does not offer a significant advantage over reduced-order DeePC in either computational cost or memory requirements. Importantly, the reduced-order DeePC with <math id="S4.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a}=r</annotation></semantics></math> preserves DeePC’s low-bias behavior for nonlinear systems (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), in contrast to SPC. When truncation is applied <math id="S4.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mk+n\leq r_{a}&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mk+n\leq r_{a}&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, computational and memory demands are further reduced, but this introduces approximation error, thereby biasing DeePC’s behavior. Thus, reduced-order DeePC admits two regimes: if all nonzero singular directions of <math id="S4.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are retained, it is algebraically equivalent to DeePC, and the optimizer remains unchanged after a variable transformation. If the SVD is truncated <math id="S4.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, the resulting optimizer generally differs—yielding lower variance but increased bias. Applying SVD and retaining only the dominant singular directions can be interpreted as fitting the data with a linear model of bounded complexity. In this sense, the choice of rank in the SVD truncation is equivalent to the choice of model complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kernel Representation:</span>
This framework was introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib145" title="" class="ltx_ref">145</a>]</cite> under the name efficient data-driven predictive control (eDDPC). It should be noted, however, that all of the approaches discussed in this section can be viewed as forms of efficient data-driven predictive control. In the eDDPC formulation, a polynomial matrix <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(z)\in\mathbb{R}^{v\times q}[z]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(z)\in\mathbb{R}^{v\times q}[z]</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex67" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex67X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex67X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex67X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(z)=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1}(z)\\
r_{2}(z)\\
\vdots\\
r_{v}(z)\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1,0}+r_{1,1}z+\cdots+r_{1,l}z^{l}\\
r_{2,0}+r_{2,1}z+\cdots+r_{2,l}z^{l}\\
\vdots\\
r_{v,0}+r_{v,1}z+\cdots+r_{v,l}z^{l}\end{bmatrix},\quad r_{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>r</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}(z)=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1}(z)\\
r_{2}(z)\\
\vdots\\
r_{v}(z)\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1,0}+r_{1,1}z+\cdots+r_{1,l}z^{l}\\
r_{2,0}+r_{2,1}z+\cdots+r_{2,l}z^{l}\\
\vdots\\
r_{v,0}+r_{v,1}z+\cdots+r_{v,l}z^{l}\end{bmatrix},\quad r_{i,j}\in\mathbb{R}^{q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p9.4" class="ltx_p">where can be expressed as <math id="S4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(z)=\mathcal{R}_{0}+\mathcal{R}_{1}z+...+\mathcal{R}_{l}z^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>l</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(z)=\mathcal{R}_{0}+\mathcal{R}_{1}z+...+\mathcal{R}_{l}z^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{v\times q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{v\times q}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the behavioral systems theory, forward shift operator <math id="S4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> acts on a signal <math id="S4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in\mathbb{R}^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in\mathbb{R}^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{j}w_{k}=w_{k+j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{j}w_{k}=w_{k+j}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S4.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> moves the trajectory <math id="S4.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> steps forward in time. One can evaluate <math id="S4.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> at the shift operator <math id="S4.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain an operator on the signal <math id="S4.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\sigma)w=\mathcal{R}_{0}w_{k}+\mathcal{R}_{1}w_{k+1}+...+\mathcal{R}_{l}w_{k+l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\sigma)w=\mathcal{R}_{0}w_{k}+\mathcal{R}_{1}w_{k+1}+...+\mathcal{R}_{l}w_{k+l}</annotation></semantics></math>. The Fundamental Lemma characterizes the behavior of the LTI system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) as the image (column space) of the Hankel matrix. Equivalently, the same system admits a kernel (null-space) representation given by:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex68" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex68X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex68X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex68X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{B}=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}(w^{d}))=\operatorname{\ker}(\mathcal{R}(\sigma))," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{B}=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}(w^{d}))=\operatorname{\ker}(\mathcal{R}(\sigma)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p9.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\sigma)w^{d}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\sigma)w^{d}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for any signal <math id="S4.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that belongs to the LTI system. We now use the following results to retrieve the kernel representation <math id="S4.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{\ker}(\mathcal{R}(\sigma))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{\ker}(\mathcal{R}(\sigma))</annotation></semantics></math> of the LTI system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). By exploiting the kernel structure of the Hankel matrix, one can obtain a full column-rank matrix whose image is equal to the finite-length behavior of the LTI system.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Kernel Representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib146" title="" class="ltx_ref">146</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as the signal of the controllable LTI system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), let <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{H}_{M}(w^{d}))=mM+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{H}_{M}(w^{d}))=mM+n</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficients of the polynomial operator <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, are given by elements of full row-rank matrix <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{pM-n\times qM}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{pM-n\times qM}</annotation></semantics></math> as</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex69" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex69X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex69X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{M}=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}\\
r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
r_{pM-n,0}&amp;r_{pM-n,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{pM-n,M-1}\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{M}=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}\\
r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
r_{pM-n,0}&amp;r_{pM-n,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{pM-n,M-1}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which is the basis of the left null space of the Hankel matrix as <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{M}\mathcal{H}_{M}(w^{d})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{M}\mathcal{H}_{M}(w^{d})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Non-Parametric Representation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib146" title="" class="ltx_ref">146</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmcorollary2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> as the signal of the controllable LTI system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), let <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\geq M\geq\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\geq M\geq\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{H}_{M}(w^{d}))=mM+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{H}_{M}(w^{d}))=mM+n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is a signal of the LTI system if and only if there exists a unique vector <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{mZ+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{mZ+n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E23X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E23X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{P}\beta=w," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{P}\beta=w,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the full column-rank matrix <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\operatorname{null}(\Gamma)\in\mathbb{R}^{qZ\times mZ+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>null</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\operatorname{null}(\Gamma)\in\mathbb{R}^{qZ\times mZ+n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the full row-rank matrix <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in\mathbb{R}^{pZ-n\times qZ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in\mathbb{R}^{pZ-n\times qZ}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex70" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex70X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex70X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Gamma=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}&amp;&amp;\\
r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}&amp;&amp;\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;&amp;\\
r_{pM-n,0}&amp;r_{pM-n,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{pM-n,M-1}&amp;&amp;\\
&amp;r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}&amp;\\
&amp;r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}&amp;\\
&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\\
&amp;r_{p,0}&amp;r_{p,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{p,M-1}&amp;\\
&amp;&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;r_{p,0}&amp;r_{p,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{p,M-1}\\
\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">n</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mn mathsize="0.700em">2</mn><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">0</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.700em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">r</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Gamma=\begin{bmatrix}r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}&amp;&amp;\\
r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}&amp;&amp;\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;&amp;\\
r_{pM-n,0}&amp;r_{pM-n,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{pM-n,M-1}&amp;&amp;\\
&amp;r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}&amp;\\
&amp;r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}&amp;\\
&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\\
&amp;r_{p,0}&amp;r_{p,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{p,M-1}&amp;\\
&amp;&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\ddots&amp;\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;r_{1,0}&amp;r_{1,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{1,M-1}\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;r_{2,0}&amp;r_{2,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{2,M-1}\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
&amp;&amp;&amp;r_{p,0}&amp;r_{p,1}&amp;\cdots&amp;r_{p,M-1}\\
\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmcorollary2.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\mathcal{H}_{Z}(w^{d})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\mathcal{H}_{Z}(w^{d})=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmcorollary2.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the elements of the matrix <math id="Thmcorollary2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in Corollary 1.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing the Fundamental Lemma and Corollary 2 under <math id="S4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=K=T_{ini}+N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=K=T_{ini}+N</annotation></semantics></math>, both provide a non-parametric representation of the finite-length behavior of the controllable LTI system. This is true since <math id="S4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}_{L}(w^{\text{data}}))=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{P})=\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}_{L}(w^{\text{data}}))=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{P})=\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In Corollary 2 (Non-Parametric Representation []). ‣ IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) is an efficient non-parametric representation since the Fundamental Lemma requires <math id="S4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq(m+1)(K+n)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq(m+1)(K+n)-1</annotation></semantics></math> data points, whereas Corollary 2 requires <math id="S4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq(m+1)(\ell+n+1)-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq(m+1)(\ell+n+1)-1</annotation></semantics></math>. If the minimum <math id="S4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen in both cases, Corollary 2 requires <math id="S4.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+1)(K-\ell-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+1)(K-\ell-1)</annotation></semantics></math> less samples, for any <math id="S4.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K&gt;\ell+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K&gt;\ell+1</annotation></semantics></math>. By the way, it is only a big advantage for large systems and/or large <math id="S4.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the Fundamental Lemma has infinitely many solutions for <math id="S4.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas Corollary 2 presents a unique solution <math id="S4.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{mK+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\mathbb{R}^{mK+n}</annotation></semantics></math> for each trajectory. Finally, the dimension of <math id="S4.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the number of collected data points, whereas the dimension of <math id="S4.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> increases with increasing <math id="S4.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, which helps the controller to require less computation and memory. One has <math id="S4.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rank(\mathcal{P})=rank(\mathcal{H}_{Z}(w^{d}))=mZ+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rank(\mathcal{P})=rank(\mathcal{H}_{Z}(w^{d}))=mZ+n</annotation></semantics></math> for the deterministic linear system (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In addition, for the general nonlinear system (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), <math id="S4.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> must be large enough to enhance the performance of the non-parametric representation (<a href="#S4.E23" title="In Corollary 2 (Non-Parametric Representation []). ‣ IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) on system’s behavior prediction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.3" class="ltx_p">Now, similarly to (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), one can formulate eDDPC as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib145" title="" class="ltx_ref">145</a>, <a href="#bib.bib147" title="" class="ltx_ref">147</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E24X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{\beta}^{*},\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{\beta},\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">β</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐮</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐮</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{\beta}^{*},\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{\beta},\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E24Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\mathcal{P}\beta=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\mathcal{P}\beta=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E24Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E(y,u)\leq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E(y,u)\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.4" class="ltx_p">where the data matrix <math id="S4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is full column rank and is obtained offline using Corollary 2. Moreover, similarly to (<a href="#S3.E17" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), one can partition the data matrix <math id="S4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=[\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{u_{ini}},\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{y_{ini}},\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{u},\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{y}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=[\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{u_{ini}},\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{y_{ini}},\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{u},\mathcal{P}^{\top}_{y}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> and rewrite (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>) as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex71" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex71X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex71X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex71X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\beta}^{*}=\underset{\mathbf{\beta}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathcal{P}_{y}\mathbf{\beta},\mathcal{P}_{u}\mathbf{\beta},\mathcal{P}_{u_{ini}}\mathbf{\beta}-u_{ini},\mathcal{P}_{y_{ini}}\mathbf{\beta}-y_{ini},\mathbf{\beta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>β</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛽</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\beta}^{*}=\underset{\mathbf{\beta}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathcal{P}_{y}\mathbf{\beta},\mathcal{P}_{u}\mathbf{\beta},\mathcal{P}_{u_{ini}}\mathbf{\beta}-u_{ini},\mathcal{P}_{y_{ini}}\mathbf{\beta}-y_{ini},\mathbf{\beta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex71Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex71Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE(\mathcal{P}_{y}\mathbf{\beta},\mathcal{P}_{u}\mathbf{\beta})\leq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE(\mathcal{P}_{y}\mathbf{\beta},\mathcal{P}_{u}\mathbf{\beta})\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p11.5" class="ltx_p">where is an optimization problem on <math id="S4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the computational burden depends on the dimension of <math id="S4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="mK+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">mK+n</annotation></semantics></math>, instead of the dimension of <math id="S4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, we only need to save <math id="S4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, which requires much less memory compared to the Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. eDDPC is exactly solved as DeePC, and one can achieve a closed-form solution <math id="S4.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=K_{e}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{e}^{ini}w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=K_{e}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{e}^{ini}w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> for the unconstrained QP version by setting the gradient of the quadratic cost to zero <math id="S4.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J_{\beta}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J_{\beta}=0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Comparing the reduced-order DeePC (with <math id="S4.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) and eDDPC, both are in fact equivalent to DeePC for the deterministic LTI systems <math id="S4.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{P})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}^{\prime})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{P})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}^{\prime})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, the resulting closed-loop trajectories are identical. The reduced-order DeePC and eDDPC have the same improvement in computation and memory burden as <math id="S4.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times mK+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times mK+n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p11.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times mK+n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times mK+n}</annotation></semantics></math>; however, eDDPC is a sample efficient one for large systems and/or large <math id="S4.p11.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (requires less collected data points). On the other hand, eDDPC introduces new challenges: as the system complexity <math id="S4.p11.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m,p,n,\ell)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m,p,n,\ell)</annotation></semantics></math> increases, some numerical problems are encountered when constructing the matrix <math id="S4.p11.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, taking the first <math id="S4.p11.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> rows of the matrix <math id="S4.p11.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> can sometimes result in an ill-conditioned matrix <math id="S4.p11.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., very large condition number (the ratio between the largest and smallest non-zero singular values), which might lead to errors in computing <math id="S4.p11.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=null(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=null(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. One explanation is that the kernel representation specified by the rows of <math id="S4.p11.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is not necessarily minimal. In addition, although the reduced-order DeePC may require a little bit more computation and memory for the nonlinear system (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In II Data-Driven System Representation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), it has the same behavior as DeePC on the bias-variance trade-off. However, eDDPC is not guaranteed to have the same behavior, but it can be similar to them. Thus, one may conclude that the reduced-order DeePC is a better choice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Range Space Equivalence:</span>
By exploiting the range space (column space) of the Hankel matrix, i.e., <math id="S4.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, DeePC can be reformulated to reduce computational cost. This reformulation replaces the Hankel matrix with a lower-dimensional representation of its column space, while remaining algebraically equivalent to the original problem. The derivation of this reformulation relies on the following lemma:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmlemma4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Range Space Equivalence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib148" title="" class="ltx_ref">148</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmlemma4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive-definite matrix, i.e., <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for the Hankel matrix <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}\Psi^{-1}\mathcal{H}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}\Psi^{-1}\mathcal{H}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, with <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi=I_{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi=I_{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}\mathcal{H}^{\top})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H})=\operatorname{im}(\mathcal{H}\mathcal{H}^{\top})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p13.2" class="ltx_p">Using the above lemma, one can formulate range-space DeePC as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib148" title="" class="ltx_ref">148</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E25X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\mathbf{\alpha}^{*},\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{\alpha},\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\alpha})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">α</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐮</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐮</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">σ</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\mathbf{\alpha}^{*},\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}}^{*},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{\alpha},\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptJ(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{\alpha})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E25Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\mathcal{G}\alpha=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\mathcal{G}\alpha=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E25Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E25Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E(y,u)\leq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E(y,u)\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p13.3" class="ltx_p">where the matrix <math id="S4.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=\mathcal{H}\Gamma^{-1}\mathcal{H}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times qK}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=\mathcal{H}\Gamma^{-1}\mathcal{H}^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times qK}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained offline. Moreover, under the quadratic regularization term <math id="S4.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g\|_{\Psi}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g\|_{\Psi}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for DeePC, one has the quadratic regularization term <math id="S4.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\alpha\|_{\mathcal{G}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\alpha\|_{\mathcal{G}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the range-space DeePC. Similarly to (<a href="#S4.E24" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>), the problem is rewritten to only has <math id="S4.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> as the optimization variable. Therefore, the computational burden depends on the dimension of <math id="S4.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="qK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">qK</annotation></semantics></math>, instead of the dimension of <math id="S4.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, that is <math id="S4.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition, we only need to save <math id="S4.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, which requires much less memory compared to the Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. The range-space DeePC is exactly solved as DeePC, and one can achieve a closed-form solution <math id="S4.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}=K_{r}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{r}^{ini}w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐫</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}=K_{r}^{r}\mathbf{r}+K_{r}^{ini}w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> for the unconstrained QP version by setting the gradient of the quadratic cost to zero <math id="S4.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(J_{\alpha}=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(J_{\alpha}=0)</annotation></semantics></math>. Both the range-space DeePC and the reduced-order DeePC with <math id="S4.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{a}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{a}=r</annotation></semantics></math> keeps DeePC’s behavior on the bias-variance trade-off; however, the reduced-order DeePC requires lower or same computation and memory. In addition, if <math id="S4.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert g\rVert_{\Psi}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>g</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert g\rVert_{\Psi}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly convex, there is no guarantee that <math id="S4.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\alpha\rVert_{\mathcal{G}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>α</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\alpha\rVert_{\mathcal{G}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> remains strictly convex, since if <math id="S4.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> does not have full row rank (and we only assume <math id="S4.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi\succ 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>≻</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi\succ 0</annotation></semantics></math>), then <math id="S4.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}\succeq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⪰</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}\succeq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, <math id="S4.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> has a worse condition number than <math id="S4.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. with <math id="S4.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi=I</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{cond}(\mathcal{G})=\mathrm{cond}(\mathcal{H})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>cond</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cond</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{cond}(\mathcal{G})=\mathrm{cond}(\mathcal{H})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), which can deteriorate numerical accuracy or require more iterations in optimization algorithms. Therefore, one may prefer to stay with the reduced-order DeePC as the best choice. Note that these modified efficient versions of DeePC are guaranteed to be equivalent to DeePC only when the regularization term is a positive-definite quadratic form, such as <math id="S4.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g\|_{\Psi}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g\|_{\Psi}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., an <math id="S4.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm penalty).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discrete Fourier Transform:</span>
Factorizations based on the discrete Fourier transform (DFT) of the Hankel matrix enable fast and memory-efficient computations. To exploit this in DeePC, one must employ DFT-based iterative optimization algorithms (e.g., gradient-descent variants) for the augmented cost function, thereby avoiding the need to store the Hankel matrix explicitly. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib149" title="" class="ltx_ref">149</a>]</cite> proposes a two-step procedure: first, they derive a DFT-based factorization that enables efficient matrix–vector multiplications involving the Hankel matrix; then, they integrate this factorization into DeePC by reformulating the augmented cost function and solving it with an iterative optimal control algorithm. This combination allows DeePC to be solved without storing the Hankel matrix, requiring only the implementation of a matrix–vector product operator based on the data <math id="S4.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and its DFT. Unlike SVD-based reductions, the DFT-based approach preserves the full DeePC model, and its efficiency gain arises purely from computational structure rather than from modifying the feasible set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p15.2" class="ltx_p">A straightforward approach to calculate the matrix-vector product <math id="S4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g</annotation></semantics></math> without storing the full Hankel matrix <math id="S4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(w^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(w^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> is to make use of the structure of the Hankel matrix and access its coefficients directly from the generating sequence <math id="S4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. A factorization of <math id="S4.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(w^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(w^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> likewise solves the memory problem and in addition is favorable for the speed of matrix-vector multiplication. A row permutation of <math id="S4.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(w^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(w^{d})</annotation></semantics></math> leads to the Toeplitz matrix <math id="S4.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi\in\mathbb{R}^{qK\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex72" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex72X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex72X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex72X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Xi:=\begin{bmatrix}w^{d}_{K}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T}\\
\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
w^{d}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{L}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}&amp;&amp;I_{q}\\
&amp;\iddots&amp;\\
I_{q}&amp;&amp;\end{bmatrix}\mathcal{H}(w^{d})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋰</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Xi:=\begin{bmatrix}w^{d}_{K}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T}\\
\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
w^{d}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{L}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}&amp;&amp;I_{q}\\
&amp;\iddots&amp;\\
I_{q}&amp;&amp;\end{bmatrix}\mathcal{H}(w^{d}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p15.3" class="ltx_p">which can be embedded into a circulant block-Toeplitz matrix <math id="S4.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon\in\mathbb{R}^{qT\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon\in\mathbb{R}^{qT\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi=\left[I_{qK}\ 0_{qK\times q(T-K)}\right]\Upsilon\left[I_{L}\ 0_{L\times(T-L)}\right]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn> 0</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn> 0</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi=\left[I_{qK}\ 0_{qK\times q(T-K)}\right]\Upsilon\left[I_{L}\ 0_{L\times(T-L)}\right]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex73" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon=\begin{bmatrix}w^{d}_{K}&amp;w^{d}_{K+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T}&amp;w^{d}_{1}&amp;w^{d}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{K-1}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
w^{d}_{1}&amp;w^{d}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{L}&amp;w^{d}_{L+1}&amp;w^{d}_{L+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T}\\
w^{d}_{T}&amp;w^{d}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{L-1}&amp;w^{d}_{L}&amp;w^{d}_{L+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T-1}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
w^{d}_{K+1}&amp;w^{d}_{K+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{1}&amp;w^{d}_{2}&amp;w^{d}_{3}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{K}\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo mathsize="0.900em">⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon=\begin{bmatrix}w^{d}_{K}&amp;w^{d}_{K+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T}&amp;w^{d}_{1}&amp;w^{d}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{K-1}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
w^{d}_{1}&amp;w^{d}_{2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{L}&amp;w^{d}_{L+1}&amp;w^{d}_{L+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T}\\
w^{d}_{T}&amp;w^{d}_{1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{L-1}&amp;w^{d}_{L}&amp;w^{d}_{L+1}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{T-1}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
w^{d}_{K+1}&amp;w^{d}_{K+2}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{1}&amp;w^{d}_{2}&amp;w^{d}_{3}&amp;\cdots&amp;w^{d}_{K}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Then, the matrix <math id="S4.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon</annotation></semantics></math> can be factorized as <math id="S4.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon=\tilde{F}\Lambda F^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon=\tilde{F}\Lambda F^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p16.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F:=[e^{-2\pi ikj/N}]_{k,j=1,\ldots,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:=[e^{-2\pi ikj/N}]_{k,j=1,\ldots,T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a Fourier matrix in <math id="S4.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{F}:=F\otimes I_{q}\in\mathbb{C}^{qT\times qT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{F}:=F\otimes I_{q}\in\mathbb{C}^{qT\times qT}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda:=\operatorname{diag}(\Lambda_{1},\dots,\Lambda_{T})\in\mathbb{C}^{qT\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda:=\operatorname{diag}(\Lambda_{1},\dots,\Lambda_{T})\in\mathbb{C}^{qT\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S4.Ex74" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}\Lambda_{1}\\
\vdots\\
\Lambda_{T}\end{bmatrix}:=\tilde{F}\begin{bmatrix}w_{K}\\
\vdots\\
w_{T}\\
w_{1}\\
\vdots\\
w_{K-1}\end{bmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}\Lambda_{1}\\
\vdots\\
\Lambda_{T}\end{bmatrix}:=\tilde{F}\begin{bmatrix}w_{K}\\
\vdots\\
w_{T}\\
w_{1}\\
\vdots\\
w_{K-1}\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Considering <math id="S4.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\begin{bmatrix}g^{\top}&amp;0^{\top}_{T-L}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\begin{bmatrix}g^{\top}&amp;0^{\top}_{T-L}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrix-vector product <math id="S4.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon v</annotation></semantics></math> can be efficiently computed since <math id="S4.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{-1}v=\frac{1}{T}F^{*}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{-1}v=\frac{1}{T}F^{*}v</annotation></semantics></math> can be evaluated in <math id="S4.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(T\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(T\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> time, <math id="S4.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Lambda v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Lambda v</annotation></semantics></math> can be evaluated in <math id="S4.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(qT)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(qT)</annotation></semantics></math> time, and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex75" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{F}v=(F\otimes I_{q})v=\left[\left(e^{-2\pi i/N}\right)^{kj}I_{q}\right]_{k,j=1,\ldots,T}v" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{F}v=(F\otimes I_{q})v=\left[\left(e^{-2\pi i/N}\right)^{kj}I_{q}\right]_{k,j=1,\ldots,T}v</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p17.2" class="ltx_p">can be evaluated in <math id="S4.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(qT\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(qT\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> time. As a consequence, the matrix-vector product <math id="S4.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g</annotation></semantics></math> can be efficiently calculated via the following three steps: 1) zero-padding <math id="S4.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\begin{bmatrix}g^{\top}&amp;0^{\top}_{T-L}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\begin{bmatrix}g^{\top}&amp;0^{\top}_{T-L}\end{bmatrix}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, 2) computing <math id="S4.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Upsilon v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Υ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Upsilon v</annotation></semantics></math> using DFT-based factorization, and 3) truncating the result and permuting its components <math id="S4.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the matrix-vector multiplication <math id="S4.p17.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(w^{d})g</annotation></semantics></math> has complexity <math id="S4.p17.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(qT\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(qT\log T)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Small Prime Factors)<span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Most standard implementations of DFT run significantly faster when the signal length <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> has only small prime factors (not greater than <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math>). Therefore, apart from the PE condition, this fact can be taken into account when choosing the length of the signal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Ill-Conditioning)<span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since the convergence rate of iterative solvers often hinges on a good condition number of the Hankel matrix, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib149" title="" class="ltx_ref">149</a>]</cite> suggests Broyden-Fletcher-Golfarb-Shanno (BFGS) algorithm as iterative optimization algorithm to constructs a preconditioner of the Hankel matrix iteratively and guarantee fast convergence. To reduce the effect of inherent bad conditioning of the Hankel matrix, the limited memory Broyden-Fletcher-Golfarb-Shanno (lBFGS) algorithm is used to minimize the augmented cost function. This is due to the fact that, whereas the BFGS method is a quasi-Newton method and hence does not suffer from ill-conditioning as much as the gradient method, the preconditioner is built up successively via rank-one updates. While running, the BFGS algorithm constructs a preconditioner of the Hankel matrix which is improved in every iteration step. These updates of the preconditioner can be performed efficiently with the DFT-based factorizations presented before.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p18.1" class="ltx_p">DFT-based formulation is just a matrix-free way to solve the same DeePC. When it converges, it returns exactly the same optimizer as the standard DeePC. Thus, it is identical to DeePC in bias-variance trade-off because it only accelerates the linear algebra. However, DFT is usually usefull in online DeePC since we have <math id="S4.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(qT\log T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(qT\log T)</annotation></semantics></math> for each DeePC solve. For DeePC, the reduced-order DeePC is a better option since we obtain <math id="S4.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> offline. Moreover, online numerical SVD algorithms offer a complementary route for online DeePC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib138" title="" class="ltx_ref">138</a>, <a href="#bib.bib137" title="" class="ltx_ref">137</a>]</cite>. By maintaining a low-dimensional basis and updating it as new data arrive, online reduced-order DeePC can keep each solve small and is competitive when the trajectory subspace has low numerical rank and evolves slowly. In contrast, DFT-based DeePC avoids forming Hankel matrices, making it preferable when memory is tight or the data window changes rapidly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning-based Approximation:</span>
One can decouple control performance and model fitness in DeePC such that it can be viewed as minimizing a control cost and a score cost, where the evaluation of the latter is costly due to the size of the Hankel matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib150" title="" class="ltx_ref">150</a>]</cite>. Therefore, the scoring cost function can be approximated via differentiable convex programming, where the parameters of the approximated function are learned offline. Finally, an approximate control law is formulated to minimize the summation of the control cost and the learned scoring function. Thus, one can reformulate DeePC to separate the cost function into two parts as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex76" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptO_{c}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})+O_{m}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐲</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐮</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{y}^{*},\mathbf{u}^{*})=\underset{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526ptO_{c}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})+O_{m}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p19.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.Ex77" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex77X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex77X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O_{c}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})=J(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O_{c}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})=J(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex77Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex77Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE(y,u)\leq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE(y,u)\leq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p19.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex78" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex78X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex78X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle O_{m}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})=\min_{\mathbf{g},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}\,J(\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐮</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐠</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle O_{m}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})=\min_{\mathbf{g},\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}}}\,J(\mathbf{\sigma_{u}},\mathbf{\sigma_{y}},\mathbf{g})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex78Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex78Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\\
Y_{F}\end{bmatrix}g=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>P</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>u</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>y</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636pt\begin{bmatrix}U_{P}\\
Y_{P}\\
U_{F}\\
Y_{F}\end{bmatrix}g=\begin{bmatrix}u_{ini}\\
y_{ini}\\
u\\
y\end{bmatrix}+\begin{bmatrix}\sigma_{u}\\
\sigma_{y}\\
0\\
0\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p19.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{c}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{c}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cost function of the control problem, which is distinct from and unaffected by the system dynamics. Also, <math id="S4.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is referred to as the scoring function, which evaluates the fitness of <math id="S4.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to the trajectory segments collected in the data matrix. Once a candidate trajectory <math id="S4.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is proposed, the scoring function <math id="S4.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> simply maps <math id="S4.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> to a scalar. Thus, once <math id="S4.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is assembled, <math id="S4.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> gives its score on model fitness. <math id="S4.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on the system and is not influenced by any specific cost or safety constraint. Note that <math id="S4.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,\sigma_{u},\sigma_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,\sigma_{u},\sigma_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> are not part of the control policy and only evaluate the scoring function, where the dimension of <math id="S4.p19.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the number of the collected data. Therefore, we desire to accelerate the evaluation of <math id="S4.p19.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> using a learning-based approach which approximates the scoring function with fewer variables. We denote the approximate scoring function as <math id="S4.p19.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the overall control problem becomes:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex79" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}}\,O_{c}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})+\hat{O}_{m}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.337em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}}\,O_{c}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u})+\hat{O}_{m}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{u}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p19.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p19.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping from an I/O signal <math id="S4.p19.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u,y)</annotation></semantics></math> to the value of the corresponding scoring function and is learned offline. Here, the learned <math id="S4.p19.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> is used to
substitute the original scoring function <math id="S4.p19.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math> and efficiently solve the above control problem online. We refer the interested readers to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib150" title="" class="ltx_ref">150</a>]</cite> for further details on the offline learning process of <math id="S4.p19.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math>. For this approach, the computation time is mainly determined by the size of the learned scoring function <math id="S4.p19.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{O}_{m}(y,u)</annotation></semantics></math>, and there is also a trade-off between control performance and computation time. Therefore, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib151" title="" class="ltx_ref">151</a>]</cite> uses deep learning to directly learn the DeePC policy as <math id="S4.p19.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{g}=F_{\theta}(r,w_{ini})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{g}=F_{\theta}(r,w_{ini})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is called Deep DeePC. However, the main advantage of DeePC is avoiding the offline learning process for control systems; thus, these two approaches may not be perfectly aligned with DeePC’s goal. Moreover, they introduce a new challenge for DeePC, which is learning errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal:</span>
One can use neighboring extremal (NE) adaptation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> to approximate the DeePC solution. Considering DeePC (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), we have a nominal solution <math id="S4.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u^{o},y^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u^{o},y^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> for an initial I/O trajectory <math id="S4.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{ini}^{o},y_{ini}^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{ini}^{o},y_{ini}^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> and reference trajectory <math id="S4.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{o}</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, for a new initial I/O trajectory <math id="S4.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(u_{ini},y_{ini})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u_{ini},y_{ini})</annotation></semantics></math> and/or reference trajectory <math id="S4.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, the DeePC solution is approximated by <math id="S4.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{*}=u^{o}+\delta u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{*}=u^{o}+\delta u</annotation></semantics></math> using NE adaptation. The objective is to compute <math id="S4.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta u</annotation></semantics></math> using data-enabled neighboring extremal (DeeNE) for the optimization problem (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). The resulting equation is a closed-form approximation of the DeePC solution, which helps us to reduce the computational time of DeePC.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p21.2" class="ltx_p">Considering (<a href="#S3.E18" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), the augmented cost function can be rewritten as:</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E26X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E26X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E26X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}(w_{ini},g,r,\mu)=J(w_{ini},g,r)+\mu^{T}E^{a}(g)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}(w_{ini},g,r,\mu)=J(w_{ini},g,r)+\mu^{T}E^{a}(g).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Active Constraints)<span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{a}_{g}(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{a}_{g}(g)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is full row rank.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p22.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w^{o}_{ini},g^{o},r^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w^{o}_{ini},g^{o},r^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the nominal solution for the DeePC (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>), they satisfy the following necessary optimality conditions obtained from the KKT conditions for the augmented cost function (<a href="#S4.E26" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex80" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex80X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex80X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex80X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}_{g}(w_{ini},g,r,\mu)=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}_{g}(w_{ini},g,r,\mu)=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex80Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex80Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p23.3" class="ltx_p">Now, using the above KKT conditions and the nominal solution <math id="S4.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w^{o}_{ini},g^{o},r^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w^{o}_{ini},g^{o},r^{o})</annotation></semantics></math>, one can calculate the Lagrange multiplier <math id="S4.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> online as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex81" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex81X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex81X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex81X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J_{g}(w^{o}_{ini},g^{o},r^{o})+\mu^{T}E^{a}_{g}(g^{o})=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mi>o</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J_{g}(w^{o}_{ini},g^{o},r^{o})+\mu^{T}E^{a}_{g}(g^{o})=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p23.4" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex82" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex82X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex82X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex82X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mu=-{(E^{a}_{g}{E^{a}_{g}}^{\top})}^{-1}E^{a}_{g}J^{\top}_{g}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mu=-{(E^{a}_{g}{E^{a}_{g}}^{\top})}^{-1}E^{a}_{g}J^{\top}_{g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p24.1" class="ltx_p">Note that Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Active Constraints). ‣ IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> guarantees that <math id="S4.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{a}_{g}{E^{a}_{g}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{a}_{g}{E^{a}_{g}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is invertible. Moreover, it is worth noting that <math id="S4.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=0</annotation></semantics></math> if the constraint <math id="S4.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(g^{o})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(g^{o})</annotation></semantics></math> is not active. The Lagrange multiplier is considered as the nominal Lagrange multiplier <math id="S4.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu^{o}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p25.3" class="ltx_p">Adapting to initial I/O perturbation and/or reference perturbation, DeeNE seeks to minimize the second-order variation of (<a href="#S4.E26" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) subject to linearized constraints. More specifically, given <math id="S4.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta r</annotation></semantics></math>, DeeNE solves the following optimization problem as:</p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E27X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\delta g^{*}}=\underset{\mathbf{\delta g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526pt{J}^{ne}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐠</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\delta g^{*}}=\underset{\mathbf{\delta g}}{\arg\min}\hskip 2.84526pt{J}^{ne}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E27Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE^{a}_{g}\delta g=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo><mspace style="width:1.587em;" width="1.587em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.\hskip 14.22636ptE^{a}_{g}\delta g=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p25.4" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.Ex83" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex83X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex83X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex83X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle J}^{ne}=\delta^{2}\bar{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle J}^{ne}=\delta^{2}\bar{J}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex83Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex83Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="1.988em">=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p26.3" class="ltx_p">For (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>), the augmented cost function are obtained as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex84" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex84X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex84X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex84X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}=\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}=\frac{1}{2}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}^{T}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{w_{ini}w_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}g}&amp;\bar{J}_{w_{ini}r}\\
\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;\bar{J}_{gr}\\
\bar{J}_{rw_{ini}}&amp;\bar{J}_{rg}&amp;\bar{J}_{rr}\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\delta w_{ini}\\
\delta g\\
\delta r\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex84Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex84Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\delta\mu^{T}E^{a}_{g}\delta g," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>μ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\delta\mu^{T}E^{a}_{g}\delta g,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p26.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lagrange multiplier for (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). By applying the KKT conditions to the above augmented cost function, one has</p>
<table id="S4.Ex85" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex85X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex85X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex85X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}_{\delta g}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bar{J}^{ne}_{\delta g}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex85Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex85Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta\mu\geq 0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta\mu\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p27.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem presents the proposed DeeNE to approximate the DeepC policy in the presence of initial input-output and reference perturbations.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib152" title="" class="ltx_ref">152</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the optimization problem (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). If <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, DeeNE policy</span></p>
<table id="S4.E28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E28X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta g=K^{*}_{1}\delta w_{ini}+K^{*}_{2}\delta r," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta g=K^{*}_{1}\delta w_{ini}+K^{*}_{2}\delta r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E28Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K^{*}_{1}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K^{*}_{1}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gw_{ini}}\\
0\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E28Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K^{*}_{2}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K^{*}_{2}=-\begin{bmatrix}I&amp;0\end{bmatrix}K^{o}\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gr}\\
0\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E28Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K^{o}=\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&{E^{a}_{g}}^{\top}\\
E^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></mmultiscripts></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K^{o}=\begin{bmatrix}\bar{J}_{gg}&amp;{E^{a}_{g}}^{\top}\\
E^{a}_{g}&amp;0\end{bmatrix}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">approximates the perturbed solution of the nominal DeePC (<a href="#S3.E15" title="In III Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) with initial I/O perturbation <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math> and reference perturbation <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta r</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Singularity)<span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is worth noting that <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is essential for DeeNE since it guarantees the convexity of (<a href="#S4.E27" title="In IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Active Constraints). ‣ IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{J}_{gg}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined. If <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{a}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>a</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{a}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is not full row rank, the matrix <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is singular, leading to the failure of DeeNE. This issue can be solved using constraint back-propagation algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p28.1" class="ltx_p">Using DeeNE (<a href="#S4.E28" title="In Theorem 1 (Data-Enabled Neighboring Extremal []). ‣ IV Efficient Data-Driven Optimal Policy ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a>), one can obtain <math id="S4.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}=g^{o}+\delta g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}=g^{o}+\delta g</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="S4.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{*}=U_{F}g^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{*}=U_{F}g^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under DeeNE, one has <math id="S4.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta u=K_{ne}^{r}\delta r+K_{ne}^{ini}\delta w_{ini}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta u=K_{ne}^{r}\delta r+K_{ne}^{ini}\delta w_{ini}</annotation></semantics></math>, which shows that the online computation grows linearly with the prediction horizon. DeeNE typically requires lower computation than DeePC and reduced-order DeePC because it does not need to solve an optimization problem at each time step, just applying precomputed matrices. If perturbations become large or the active constraint set changes a lot, we may need to refresh the nominal solution, at which point the advantage narrows. On the other hand, DeeNE requires saving the matrices <math id="S4.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times qT_{ini}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times qT_{ini}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times pN}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times pN}</annotation></semantics></math>, which depend on the length of collected data <math id="S4.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>; thus, the reduced-order DeePC requires less memory. However, it is easy to develop DeeNE for the reduced-order DeePC to have even less computation burden and also require less memory. Finally, DeeNE is a local approximation of the perturbed DeePC optimum; therefore, statistical bias-variance behavior is the same as DeePC. The only extra bias is algorithmic/approximation error, which is small.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Real-Time Implementation</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While data-driven optimal control policies have been extensively explored in theory and validated through simulations, their true impact is most evident when demonstrated in experimental settings and real-time applications. Deploying such methods on physical systems provides critical insights into robustness, adaptability, and scalability under practical constraints. Experimental studies have already confirmed the effectiveness of DeePC in diverse contexts, including quadcopter flight <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, synchronous motor drives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib141" title="" class="ltx_ref">141</a>]</cite>, and Li-ion batteries fast charging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib138" title="" class="ltx_ref">138</a>]</cite>. Moreover, using efficient computational techniques, we have enabled successful real-time implementations in robotic arms, soft robotic manipulators, and autonomous driving platforms. In what follows, we review representative case studies, with particular emphasis on our own experimental results, to highlight the practical capabilities of efficient DeePC in real-world systems. These studies bridge the gap between theoretical development and practical realization, underscoring efficient DeePC’s potential as a versatile paradigm for data-driven optimal policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7-DoF Robotic Arm Motion Control:</span>
We conducted an experimental study on a 7-DoF KINOVA Gen3 robotic manipulator to evaluate the performance of DeePC and DeeNE in safe reference tracking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F17" title="Fig. 17 ‣ V Real-Time Implementation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>. The system was subject to constraints including joint velocity limits, workspace boundaries, and laboratory safety considerations. The experimental task required the robot to draw the pattern “MSU” on a board while avoiding a designated unsafe region (represented by the red box) along the reference trajectory. In contrast to model-based approaches, which demand the derivation of forward and inverse kinematics for high-DoF manipulators—a process that is both complex and calibration-intensive—data-driven optimal control policies bypass these challenges by directly exploiting I/O data. For this study, data were collected from 50 randomized trajectories and organized into mosaic Hankel matrices for DeePC. Both DeePC and DeeNE were deployed in closed-loop operation and successfully satisfied the imposed constraints, as demonstrated in Fig. <a href="#S5.F18" title="Fig. 18 ‣ V Real-Time Implementation ‣ From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>. Quantitative results, Table II, show that DeePC provided accurate trajectory tracking but suffered from a computation time of 0.2 s, which exceeded the hardware sampling rate of 0.1 s. This mismatch led to delayed control updates, producing discontinuous and slowed robot motion that limits DeePC’s real-time applicability. In contrast, DeeNE reduced computation time to 0.03 s while maintaining comparable tracking accuracy, thereby enabling smooth trajectory execution and practical real-time control.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F17" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04076v1/Figs/Robotic_Arm.PNG" id="S5.F17.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/315;" width="471" height="315" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 17: </span>Safe Reference Tracking by 7-DoF Robotic Arm. Experimental results video: <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BlKTUgkAMVo</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F18" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04076v1/Figs/Outputs3D_Safe.png" id="S5.F18.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/354;" width="471" height="354" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 18: </span>Safe reference tracking for 7-DoF Robotic Arm.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Comparison of Performance and Computational Time for DeePC and DeeNE with safety guarantees</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:65.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Controller</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:65.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">RMSE</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S5.T2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:65.4pt;">
<span id="S5.T2.1.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Time (per loop)</span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
